# Selected-A public homepage

The page's HTML, typography, labels and CSS come from the completed 12ui
conversion `.12ui/solar-homepage-v1/convert-run/output/index.html` (SHA-256
`b850aefd7236f082824986d8742ddf187bbc07051dd27917c693a241dc6c47e5`). The selected
1536 × 1024 source image has SHA-256
`56a34fde279fbbd4aa3c6ecba0a3e4b0dde5105ca307b9f1ff2a4af694f797ba`.
The orbital illustration is replaced by real Three.js planets and rings; the
mountain engraving is a static decorative image beneath the transparent canvas.
`src/assets/design/solar/mountain-illustration.png` is the generated transparent
cutout; adjacent prompt files retain the material/artwork instructions. Its visible
alpha ridge is registered near source y730–995, with the faint left
endpoint visible. A modest vertical normalization matches the source ridge
height while preserving its horizontal registration.
`model/{camera,scene,surfaces}.js` derive from the companion scene kit,
with generated engraved material maps in `src/assets/design/solar/`.
The 3D planets use genuine spherical surfaces, rather than source-image
projection. Static mountain artwork is deliberate; runtime customer fixtures or
pretend interactive fallbacks are absent.

The converter selected DM Sans 400/500 and Libre Caslon Display 400 as source-font
approximations. These exact font files are bundled locally with their SIL OFL
licenses. The source URLs were Google's `dmsans/v17` and
`librecaslondisplay/v18` font families. Font synthesis is enabled only for the
converted italic hero text. Source-driven adjustments widen the hero type and
space the process icons. The feature icons are fresh SVG drawings requested after
the initial conversion; their class and label contract stays the same. Every style is scoped to `.orbit-home`.

`SolarSystem` loads the model and renderer only in the browser and awaits decoded
local material images before creating the renderer. One runtime owns a fixed camera,
its frame loop, viewport mouse input, resize/visibility listeners and GPU teardown.
The source-registered illustration box (520,60,1016,944) is preserved at all widths;
small screens scale the complete diagram rather than cropping it. Portrait screens
place it between headline and support copy. Landscape and smaller desktops use
separate text and artwork columns, sized to keep the complete diagram above the fold.

The targeting grammar comes from the observed Space Quest IV intro at
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KyiOwKRewaU&t=115s: globe-wide perpendicular axes move
to an off-center surface point, circular optics contract around that intersection,
and two coordinates resolve independently before a quiet hold. Orbit translates the
reference into independent axis acquisition (0–2.8s), contracting optics (2.8–4.6s),
a brief coordinate lock (4.6–5.3s), surface wave (5.3–7.5s), a readable finding hold
(7.5–10.8s), and release before the next 12.8-second scan. It preserves the anchored
camera and quiet colors. The reference review and native video captures remain under
the owner's `solar-retarget-20261002/reference` evidence directory.

`model/targeting-sequence.js` acquires a new camera-facing off-center point at each
scan, inverse-transforms it to the sphere's material coordinates, and holds that local
direction through the cycle. The axes and projected leader share that rotating surface
point. The shader's angular-distance wave expands from the same local direction across
the actual sphere; depth and back-face occlusion hide its far hemisphere. Derivative-
aware line width keeps small targets legible without drawing outside the silhouette.

`project-targeting.ts` renders one finding, rather than a permanent label list. Genuine
source-plate X/Y coordinates resolve independently, briefly freeze their locked values,
and fade as the X readout opens into an annotation above the target silhouette. Customer
signal, Priorities and Follow-up review appear successively, hold, and clear before the
next acquisition. Measured text and pixel gutters keep labels inside the artwork. Digits
are decorative drawing coordinates, never customer scores, records or live announcements.
Selected orbital planes, guides, optics and readouts fade on different schedules; the
finding hold leaves two quiet orbital planes while the other construction lines recede.

`angular-momentum.ts` converts mouse movement anywhere in the viewport into angular
velocity. Each line plane rotates independently about its fixed center; pointer stops
let velocity decay smoothly, and reverse sweeps impart reverse momentum. Each angular
input is bounded at 0.30 radians. The root, camera, terrain and planet-group position
never follow the pointer. Planet textures still spin and satellites retain small bounded
natural arcs. There is no absolute cursor-position target. Touch input preserves native
scrolling; the decorative scene never handles keyboard navigation, picking or clicks.
Events and rendered frames advance momentum on the same monotonic clock. Existing
velocity is integrated before adding each movement, so a late event never inherits
the previous frame's elapsed time or loses its coast to a preceding rendering stall.

There is no manual motion control or retained manual-pause state. Only the system's
reduced-motion preference freezes the complete WebGL/overlay frame; changing that
preference freezes/resumes cleanly. Hidden tabs cancel frame scheduling and clear pending
input velocity; restoration resets the clock without advancing hidden phases. Unmount
aborts preloading, removes input/resize/media/context listeners and disposes owned geometry,
materials and shader textures. Context loss retains the truthful unavailable state.

Identity name, exact configured description, optional local identity image and
credit visibility still come from the existing public configuration mount.
Sign-in links use the native route. WebGL failure reports that the illustration
is unavailable and leaves the real page and sign-in intact. No customer API,
provider, cookie or external browser asset is needed by this scene.

The generated rock engraving maps, static mountain engraving, reconstructed arcs, source
fonts and fresh process SVGs are artistic approximations, rather than a claim of pixel
identity. The earlier procedural noise atlas is removed. Planet material images are
preloaded with abort support and only scene-owned GPU textures are disposed; no
untextured placeholder is displayed as a finished illustration. The
unchanged product contract still requires its independent provider, deployment
and complete-release acceptance evidence. Homepage checks alone do not establish
those outcomes.

Verification belongs in a complete disposable source copy. The primary workspace
must not run dev, migrate, seed, build or verify: some existing scripts explicitly seed
`.wrangler/state` despite ORBIT_TEST_STATE. Read-only typecheck and an owned browser
against an already-running preview are permitted. Root owns the narrow replacement of
legacy tests for the intentionally removed camera/action behavior. Required copy gates
include typecheck, build, the retained solar/privacy/failure/browser checks, and full
`pnpm verify`; homepage screenshots do not establish full contract or provider acceptance.
